<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#amharic">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amharic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amharic language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amharic is a Semitic language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez script</a>, and the working language of the Ethiopian federal government. It was the language of the court and administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its imposition in schools and government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> during the federation of the 1950s was one of the grievances that fed Eritrean nationalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amharic.jpg" alt="Amharic" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pikkupapupata · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethiopia_-_Amharic-speaking_regions.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ge'ez, the language of Aksumite inscriptions, became the liturgical and literary language of the Ethiopian Orthodox Church, and its script, which the Aksumites developed into a fully vocalised alphabet, is still used to write <b>Amharic</b>, Tigrinya and other languages today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Derg, 'committee' or 'council' in <b>Amharic</b>, grew out of the mutinies of early 1974.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On another view the Semitic languages of the two countries form a branch of their own, with Ge'ez, Tigrinya and Tigre in its northern half and <b>Amharic</b>, Argobba, Harari and the Gurage languages in its southern.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Tizita (also Tezeta; <b>Amharic</b> ትዝታ) means memory, nostalgia or longing.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tizita">Tizita</a></li><li><blockquote>Haile Selassie's representative steadily curtailed Eritrean autonomy: political parties and independent newspapers were suppressed, <b>Amharic</b> was imposed, and the Eritrean flag was replaced by Ethiopia's in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/eritrea-federation">Eritrea (federation)</a></li></ul></section>
